  "abstract": "We introduce UniWALM, a world-action-language model that jointly learns video, action, and language in a shared representation, with each modality serving as both conditioning signal and supervision target. Our central claim: language, when generated by the model, improves manipulation and serves as the policy's working memory. UniWALM emits a compact textual memory alongside the subtask in the same denoising pass as action, and re-consumes it on the next step --- closing a language-as-memory loop without an external planner or memory bank. On RMBench's memory-dependent M(N) subset (decisions depend on N task-relevant past observations), UniWALM reaches 33.5% without any pretrained language or reasoning model and without keyframes, improving over same-backbone FastWAM (13.3%) by +20.2 points and beating Mem-0's privileged keyframe setup (28.5%). On a real-world Find-the-Item task, generating textual memory lifts success from 11.1% (FastWAM, which shares our video backbone but has no language generation) to 61.1%, while state-of-the-art VLAs stay at or below chance (<=33.3%).",
