  "abstract": "Autonomous agents that operate over long horizons need a persistent memory of their surroundings. That memory should be structured and easily revisited and updated. 3D scene graphs are a natural structured form of such spatial memory, but existing scene graph mapping systems rely on depth cameras or LiDAR. We present LEXI-SG, a dense monocular mapping system that builds open-vocabulary 3D scene graphs from RGB input alone. LEXI-SG uses the semantic priors of foundation models to partition the image stream into rooms. Each room is reconstructed once by a feed-forward model after it has been fully observed, avoiding the scale inconsistencies of sliding-window approaches. Revisited rooms are recognized and consolidated into a single reconstruction, a Sim(3) room-level factor graph keeps the reconstruction globally consistent, and objects are stored relative to their parent room. On Habitat-Matterport 3D and egocentric office sequences, LEXI-SG improves trajectory estimation and dense reconstruction over feed-forward SLAM baselines and is competitive in open-vocabulary segmentation.",
