  "title": "MemJEPA: Learning What a Latent World-Model Planner Must Remember",
  "authors": [
   "Steven Yang"
  ],
  "abstract": "Latent world models such as DINO-WM and LeWorldModel (LeWM) plan by imagining the outcomes of candidate actions from their last few camera frames. However, they fail after what the robot controls has been out of view for longer than that. We introduce MemJEPA to address this: it carries a recurrent state through the imagination of the cross-entropy method (CEM) planner and trains it on simulated data. MemJEPA is trained on 1) erasure, which removes the controlled entity from the input frames but keeps it in the prediction targets, and 2) counterfactual training, which replays recorded moments with altered actions to teach the model about actions it never took. On reaching, pushing and navigation tasks with LeWM's frozen encoder and the entity hidden, MemJEPA trained with erasure succeeds about 78% of the time, against 13% for LeWM's predictor fine-tuned on the same erased clips without memory, and loses nothing when the entity is visible. Erasure training allows the model to remember even during occlusion: trained on ordinary clips, the same recurrent state model does not perform well with the entity hidden.",
