  "title": "Proprioceptive Surprise-Based Episodic Memory for Test-Time Adaptation in Long-Horizon Manipulation",
  "authors": [
   "Dominic Rosch-Grace"
  ],
  "abstract": "A significant open challenge in robot learning is the development of policies that can adapt through interaction with unpredictable real-world environments. We explore proprioceptive prediction error as a signal for test-time memory writes. Inspired by evidence that dopaminergic prediction error signals modulate the strength of episodic memory formation, we use this signal to guide test-time adaptation of a low-rank episodic memory tensor within a pretrained vision-language-action model, drawing loosely on the fast episodic system of complementary learning systems theory. We introduce SOAP (Surprise-based Online Adaptation using Proprioception), a family of write rules that vary which aspects of the surprise gradient they retain, and evaluate it on LIBERO-Long across three repeats of 500 rollouts. SOAP-Direction, which retains only the gradient’s direction by normalizing it to a fixed norm, achieves a 95.4% success rate against a 93.2% baseline (+2.2 percentage points, 95% CI 0.7 to 3.7), and outperforms random directions at equal gradient norm (93.9%). SOAP-Full, which retains both direction and magnitude, reaches 94.2%, within noise of the baseline. SOAP-Direction was identified in an exploratory analysis and requires held-out replication, but suggests that the gradient’s direction may be more informative than its magnitude. All results are obtained under 4-bit quantization on a consumer-grade NVIDIA RTX 4070 Super, indicating feasibility in compute-constrained settings where high control frequency is required.",
