  "abstract": "Memory-dependent manipulation requires policies to act on information that is no longer visible in the current observation. We introduce T²Mem, which builds test-time memory directly into a pretrained vision-language-action (VLA) policy, without external reasoning models or memory-specific annotations. T²Mem encodes observation history into compact fast weights through online self-supervised updates, and an observation-grounded interface between the vision-language backbone and the action expert reads from and writes to this memory. Alternating memory–policy learning lets action supervision shape what memory retains, while giving each component a fixed counterpart during optimization. On the 16 RoboMME tasks, T²Mem raises average success from 17.9% to 56.8% over the memory-free base policy, the best among all non-privileged methods, while running 3×faster than the strongest benchmark baseline. Counterfactual interventions show that its decisions depend on the specific content stored in memory.",
